verdicts:
REFUTED (minor): The mechanics are as described, but this is the behaviour the spec asks for, and the app says so before and after the click. It is not a wrong result.

1. The spec's zeroSemantics "AS THE ACTIVE ACCOUNT" section says that when activeRule is 0, "the trigger becomes AutoSwitchSettings.clampedThreshold(defaultThreshold)". Its manualSwitchRule "AFTER THE COOLDOWN" section says auto-switch "treats the active Manual only account as if it had the default threshold (for example 90%)". leaveAtThreshold (AutoSwitchEngine.swift:501-503) returns exactly that, and plan() uses it at line 277. The code matches the spec word for word.

2. The spec's line "This never, on its own, moves you off it" means something narrower than the finding reads into it. It sits against the rejected design where 0 "counts as reached at any reading", which would move you off whatever your usage. The same spec section defines the promised behaviour with the caption text "Auto-switch moves you off it at 90%". So being moved at the default is the stated contract, not a breach of it. The "rejected" entry about pulling a live login is the same point: setting Manual only must not force a move by itself.

3. The founder is warned before he clicks. The menu's tooltip for a Manual only row (thresholdHelp) reads "while you're on it, auto-switch moves you off it at the default threshold (90%)". After the change, the row caption and the CLI confirmation say the same. The 90% figure is accurate: he is moved at the next check because he is past 90%.

4. The scenario only happens under narrow conditions, all at once:
- auto-switch is on;
- the active account had its own threshold above the default;
- current usage sits between the default and that old value;
- no switch in the last 300 s;
- another account is at or below its ceiling, 80% for a 90% default, so there is somewhere to move.

5. The result is identical to picking "Default (90%)" or "90%" from the same menu at 93% usage. That already behaves this way in 1.4, and the spec deliberately rejected remembering the old custom value ("a hidden value that would come back into force later").

The only thing left is wording. When usage is already past the default, the caption and CLI could say "at the next check" instead of "at 90%". That is minor polish, not a defect a user would read as a wrong result.
